  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1230 AM MDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nAn upper ridge will build in across the region on Saturday, giving \nus drier and warmer conditions as weaker westerly flow sets in aloft. \nTemperatures will increase to slightly above average, with highs \nover the plains reaching the low-mid 80s. These conditions will \nrepeat on Sunday, with the entire region staying dry until Sunday \nevening at the earliest.\n\nLate Sunday and into next week, an upper trough will move onto the \nwest coast, bringing plenty of tropical moisture with it from the \nsouthwest. Widespread showers will move into the Continental Divide \nby early Monday morning, spreading to the rest of the CWA by the \nmiddle of the week. The main forecast challenge will be the timing \nand extent of the precipitation coverage, influenced by the movement \nof the incoming trough. In short, models have expressed a lot of \nvariability on how much moisture we get, and how long it sticks \naround. The initial wave is still expected to come on Monday, but \ndeterministic guidance has alternated between relatively wet and dry \nsolutions. The wettest version of the forecast shows the upper low \nmoving into our region by Wednesday, picking up moisture from the \nincoming remnants of hurricane Polo and moving along the southern \nColorado border. This would bring ample moisture and widespread \nprecipitation to our area, pretty consistently for most of next \nweek. Latest model runs have started picking up on this, though the \nspeed and evolution of the upper flow pattern is still lacking in \nsome consistency. For now, stuck close to NBM POPs for next week. In \ngeneral, we will see an uptick in precipitation coverage between \nMonday and Tuesday, with higher POPs spreading from west to east, \nwith less certainty on Wednesday and onwards.\n\nSomething worthy of note: If the 'wet' solution amongst the long-\nterm guidance verifies, there are hints at a strong closed low, \nfueled with remnant tropical cyclone moisture, spinning along the \nsouthern Colorado border through the back half of next week. This \nwould bring a LOT of precipitation to our area, and sustained over \nseveral days at that. The lower elevations would receive a lot of \nrain, with snow over the peaks of our higher mountains. This could \nlead to notable impacts, so models will have to be tracked as we get \ncloser to date, to see what kinds of potential impacts we could be \ndealing with.",
